block mapping and numbering
===========================

A block number means four different things in this system depending on
who is holding it, and every layer between them applies a transformation
of its own.  Nearly everything that went wrong while getting Micronix to
boot was two layers disagreeing about which number they had, and none of
those failures looked like what it was: they looked like corrupt disks,
missing files, or permission errors.

The four numbers are:

    filesystem block   what an inode holds and the superblock counts.
                       Block 0 is the boot block, block 1 the superblock.

    driver block       the same number, on its way to becoming a place -
                       skewed, offset by the reserved tracks, and split
                       into a cylinder, a head and a sector.

    physical sector    cylinder, head and sector, as the controller and
                       the platter understand it.

    file offset        where an image file keeps it, which is a fifth
                       thing again and depends on who wrote the file.

Getting from any one of these to any other is easy.  Knowing which one
you are holding is the whole problem.


the floppy: what the driver does
--------------------------------

sys/dj.c, sio(), is the entire mapping and is worth reading in full:

	D[5] = (sec / sp->cylinder) + sp->toff;	/* cylinder */
	D[6] = sec % spt;			/* sector */

	if (dev & ALT) {			/* skew */
		D[6] <<= 1;
		if (!(spt & 1) && D[6] >= spt)
			D[6]++;
		D[6] %= spt;
	}

	if (d->flags & ORG1)
		D[6]++;				/* soft sectors count from 1 */

	if (sp->ds && (sec % sp->cylinder) >= spt)
		D[6] |= VERSO;			/* the other side */

Four separate adjustments, each of which can be got wrong on its own:

toff, the track offset.  Reserved tracks are expressed here as an offset
added to every cylinder, not as blocks subtracted from every block
number.  Every Morrow format in the table has toff 2; the IBM, Xerox and
Osborne formats have 0.  Two cylinders is 40 blocks on a double sided
five inch disk, which is exactly the reserved area, and it is also
exactly what the .trim20k images have had removed from the front.  Same
quantity, three different ways of saying it.

The skew.  This one belongs to the driver and not to the medium, which
matters because it is easy to bake it into both.  A raw image holds its
sectors in sector number order; the driver maps a block to a sector and
then asks for that sector.  Doing it in both places maps every block
twice and lands somewhere unrelated.  The proof is the second level boot:
at its own place in the file it begins "ld sp,0100 / call 0a67 / jp 0000",
a program's entry point, and one skew further along the same bytes start
in the middle of an instruction.

ORG1.  Soft sectored formats number their sectors from 1 and hard
sectored ones from 0.  This is not an independent fact - see below - but
it is applied here as a separate +1 and can be lost on its own.

The side.  A double sided disk puts the second head's sectors at the top
of the cylinder, so a cylinder is two tracks.  Which brings us to the
mistake that cost the most time.

	A cylinder is not a track.  A forty cylinder double sided disk
	and an eighty cylinder single sided disk both have eighty
	tracks, both hold 800 sectors, and both report "tracks: 80" to
	the controller.  Nothing in the track count tells them apart.
	What tells them apart is where cylinder boundaries fall: with
	two heads cylinder 2 is block 40, with one head it is block 20.

The 1.6 standalone is forty cylinders, double sided.  Three independent
things say so: the second level boot asks for cylinder 2 and the
filesystem starts at block 40, the superblock's fsize is 760, and the
format table entry with 760 blocks is the double sided one.


the floppy: how the driver decides which format
-----------------------------------------------

djopen() picks an entry out of specs[], and there are two quite different
routes into that table.

Hard sectored disks are looked up.  The driver reads sector 0 and takes
byte 5c as a configuration byte, then searches specs[] for a matching
config:

	if (s->dchar & HARD) {
		sp = &specs[DEFSPECS];
		if (s->config)
			for (S = specs; S->config != END; S++)
				if (S->config == s->config)
					sp = S;
	}

Soft sectored disks are computed, from the sector length and a few
adjustments:

	sp = &specs[s->slength & 3];
	if (dev & FIVE) {
		sp += FIVEBASE;
		if ((dev & DOUBSIDE) || s->config == DOUBSIDE)
			sp += 5;
	}
	if (s->spt == 9 && s->slength == S512)
		sp += 2;

FIVEBASE is 16, and index 16 onward is the Xerox, Osborne and IBM block -
every one of which has toff 0.  So the eight Morrow five inch formats,
which are indices 8 through 15 and all have toff 2, are reachable only
through the hard sectored lookup.  A Morrow disk that is reported as soft
sectored cannot be identified correctly, no matter what else is right.

That is worth stating plainly because it inverts the intuition: hard or
soft sectored is not a small detail about how the disk was formatted, it
is the switch that decides whether the driver asks the disk what it is or
guesses from a sector size.

And the disk does know.  Byte 5c of sector 0 of the 1.6 standalone is d0,
which selects

	{0xD0, 1, 10, 20, 1, 40, 2, 512, 760}
	 config ds spt cyl spb ncyl toff bps size

forty cylinders, double sided, ten 512 byte sectors, track offset two,
760 blocks - agreeing exactly with everything measured the hard way.


what this cost, and what it looked like
---------------------------------------

The simulator's sense reported drive characteristics without the hard
sectored bit.  One bit.  The chain from there:

	soft sectored			(what we said)
	-> no configuration lookup	(so the disk is never asked)
	-> index 18, an IBM format	(what the arithmetic lands on)
	-> toff 0 instead of 2		(the only field that then matters)
	-> every read two cylinders low

The superblock is at cylinder 2 sector 2 and it read cylinder 0 sector 2.
The root inode block is at cylinder 2 sector 4 and it read cylinder 0
sector 4.  Right sector, right head, wrong cylinder, every time, by a
constant.

What that looked like from the outside was none of the above.  The kernel
booted, printed its banner, and then failed to exec /etc/init three times
and stopped.  /etc/init was present and intact.  Tracing into the exec
showed access() being handed a mode of 0141000 - allocated, directory,
sticky, no permissions - where the real inode 65 is 0110100, and
correctly refusing it with EACCES.  The kernel was right about everything
it was given; it was reading file data and being told it was an inode.

	When a filesystem looks corrupt, suspect the mapping before the
	data.  An inode that reads as a sticky directory with no
	permission bits is not a damaged inode, it is not an inode.


the hard disk: a different trick entirely
-----------------------------------------

sys/mw.c does not skew and has no track offset.  It rotates:

	cyl  = (blk / spc + tracks / 2) mod tracks
	head = (blk mod spc) / spt
	sec  = (blk mod spc) mod spt

Block 0 is the middle cylinder.  Read that backwards and physical
cylinder 0 - the only place the rom's boot looks - is at the halfway mark
of the filesystem's blocks: 5236 of 10404 on the five meg drive, 10405 of
20808 on the ten, 15606 of 31212 on the sixteen.

The likely reason is seek distance: the superblock and the inodes live at
low block numbers, so putting block 0 in the middle of the platter halves
the average seek to them.  The side effect is that the boot area is in
the middle of the block space rather than at either end, where no
reserved-area mechanism can protect it - mkfs only knows how to exclude
blocks at the end of a device, for swap.  Hence installboot, and the file
that owns cylinder 0 so the free list never offers it.


image files: a fifth number
---------------------------

Three kinds of file in this tree, and they are not interchangeable.

Micronix floppy raw images are the whole disk, sectors in sector number
order, reserved area included.  The .trim20k variants beside them have
had the first 40 blocks removed, which is exactly the reserved area, so
the filesystem starts at block 0 and the host tools can read it - and a
loader is precisely what is not in them.

IMD floppy images carry their own geometry per track, including the
sector map and the sector size, which is why an eight inch disk can have
26 sectors of 128 on track 0 and 15 of 512 everywhere else.

Simulator hard disk files have a 2048 byte label - magic d15cc0de, sector
size, cylinders, heads, sectors per track - and the data after it in
physical order, cylinder then head then sector.  So a Micronix filesystem
is not a drive file: filesystem block B lives at

	label + ((cyl * heads + head) * spt + sec) * 512

with the rotation above applied to get cyl, head and sec.  Writing a
filesystem straight into a drive file is not off by an offset, it is
shuffled, and nothing will read it.

This is also why installboot seeking to 512 * blkno is right on Micronix
and would be wrong on the host: there it is talking to /dev/m5a and the
kernel does the rotation on the way past.


rules that would have saved the time
------------------------------------

Ask the medium what it is.  The disk carries a configuration byte at
sector 0 byte 5c and the controller will tell you the sector size, the
sidedness and the sectoring.  Every hour lost here was spent deducing
something the disk was willing to say.

One fact, several hats.  Hard versus soft sectoring is the same fact as
the sector base is the same fact as whether ORG1 applies.  If you learn
one, set all of them from it, and they cannot drift apart.

Count the words carefully.  Track, cylinder and side are three words and
two of them are sometimes the same number.  Sector, block and record are
three more.

Skew belongs to exactly one layer.  Decide which, write it down, and
never apply it twice.

A reserved area has three spellings: blocks removed from the front of an
image, a track offset added to every cylinder, and nothing at all with a
file owning the blocks instead.  All three are in use here.

Read the byte, not the comment.  The monitor's read table says "sector 1,
head 0, cyl 0" and the byte is db 0.  The firmware's tables say which
sector a format starts at and the comment above them is the only place it
is written down.


